# Technical architecture rule

This project is a modular monolith:

- Next.js
- TypeScript
- PostgreSQL
- Prisma
- Docker Desktop for local PostgreSQL

The domain model is the foundation of the product. `docs/domain_model.md`, `docs/technical_architecture.md`, and `docs/review_website_north_star_for_cursor.md` are authoritative.

Evaluation is the core judgment object. A written review is one part of an evaluation, not the whole model.

The database stores canonical evidence and context. Aggregates such as Review Landscape are derived in `src/lib/aggregation` from published evaluations only. Do not store landscape percentages, homepage scores, or a canonical global score.

Do not prematurely introduce microservices, Redis, Elasticsearch, a separate backend, message queues, Kubernetes, or a public REST API.

Prefer server components for reads and server actions for mutations. Keep domain logic out of presentation components.

Use Prisma migrations for schema changes. The schema lives at `prisma/schema.prisma`.

## Schema reconciliation already decided

- `WorkType` uses `TV_SERIES`, not `TELEVISION`.
- `Stance` is `POSITIVE | MIXED | NEGATIVE`.
- `ReviewSource` is `INTERNAL | IMPORTED | MIGRATED`.
- `OwnershipContext` uses `OWNED`, not `PURCHASED`.
- Reviewer profiles and evaluations use `onDelete: Restrict` so account removal does not cascade-delete review history.
- Full evaluation revision history and an audit log are intentionally deferred. Evaluation identity stays stable across edits.
- A reviewer has one evaluation per work. Edits update that evaluation.

When an architectural change is proposed, identify its consequences before implementing it.

## Product voice

Describe evidence. "Reviewers disagree about execution." Do not appoint the site as the judge of the work. Show sample size. A small sample is not a consensus. Enjoyment and execution stay independent. Lens and standard stay independent.
